    {
      "id": "cdf1d3a6-2fdd-42f4-a8b3-34332629de58",
      "time": 1792424858.5380797,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::OpenCodeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "",
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "tool_call_id": "call_95e83f43a5aa422b9fc66897",
            "title": "find codespace -iname \"*09*model-basics*\" 2>/dev/null",
            "raw_input": {
              "command": "find /workspaces/codespace -iname \"*09*model-basics*\" 2>/dev/null",
              "cwd": "/workspaces/codespace"
            },
            "raw_output": {
              "metadata": {
                "output": "/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/solutions/09-lecture-model-basics.ipynb\n/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/solutions/.ipynb_checkpoints/09-lecture-model-basics-checkpoint.ipynb\n/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/09-model-basics.ipynb\n/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/.ipynb_checkpoints/09-model-basics-checkpoint.ipynb\n",
                "exit": 0.0,
                "truncated": false
              },
              "output": "/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/solutions/09-lecture-model-basics.ipynb\n/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/solutions/.ipynb_checkpoints/09-lecture-model-basics-checkpoint.ipynb\n/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/09-model-basics.ipynb\n/workspaces/codespace/notebooks/.ipynb_checkpoints/09-model-basics-checkpoint.ipynb\n"
            },
            "locations": [
              "/workspaces/codespace"
            ],
            "kind": "execute",
            "status": "completed"
          },
          {
            "raw_output": {
              "metadata": {
                "truncated": false
              },
              "output": "```yaml\nkernelspec:\n  display_name: R 4.5.3 (xr)\n  language: R\n  name: xr\nlanguage_info:\n  codemirror_mode: ''\n  file_extension: .R\n  mimetype: text/x-R\n  name: R\n  nbconvert_exporter: ''\n  pygments_lexer: r\n  version: 4.5.3\n```\n\n### Cell 0\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000001'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n# Model basics: where do \"model parameters\" come from?\n\nIn the previous regression lecture we fit `realinc ~ educ` and calculated two numbers: an intercept of about \u2212$14,000 and a slope of about $3,600. Today we will discover where those numbers came from.\n\nThis lesson is adapted from Chapter 23, \"Model basics\", of:\n\n> Wickham, H. & Grolemund, G. *R for Data Science.* <https://r4ds.had.co.nz/model-basics.html>\n\nLicensed CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US. The original uses simulated data (`sim1`). We use our GSS data instead.```\n\n### Cell 1\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000002'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Setup```\n\n### Cell 2\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 1\nid: '00000003'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nlibrary('tidymodels')\n\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)```\n\n### Cell 3\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000004'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## The data\n\nLoad the GSS data set from the regression lecture and drop the rows with missing values.```\n\n### Cell 4\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 2\nid: '00000005'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ngss = read.csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/gss_subset.csv')\n\ngss <- gss |> drop_na(realinc, educ)\n\ngss |> glimpse()```\n\n### Cell 5\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000006'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nIf we plot every person, the points pile up on top of each other (i.e. there is lots of overplotting). `geom_jitter()` moves the points around a bit and `alpha` makes them transparent. Together, they show the trend in the data.```\n\n### Cell 6\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 3\nid: '00000007'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(gss, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_jitter(alpha = 0.02, width = 0.3)```\n\n### Cell 7\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 00000008\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nStill hard to see. For visualization in this notebook, we'll plot the **mean** income at each education level. \n\n**The model we create will still use every data point, not just the mean value for each education level**.```\n\n### Cell 8\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 4\nid: 00000009\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nincome_by_educ <- gss |>\n    group_by(educ) |>\n    summarize(realinc = mean(realinc))\n\nincome_by_educ |> head()```\n\n### Cell 9\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 5\nid: 0000000a\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) + geom_point()```\n\n### Cell 10\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000000b\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## A model family\n\nLet's \"fit\" a line to the data. A line takes the form:\n\n$$ \\text{realinc} = a_1 + a_2 \\times \\text{educ} $$\n\n- $a_1$ is the **intercept**. It moves the line up and down.\n- $a_2$ is the **slope**. It tilts the line.\n\nBut, what values should we use for $a_1$ and $a_2$? Let's start by guessing. We'll make 250 random combinations.```\n\n### Cell 11\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 5\nid: 0000000c\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nset.seed(512)\n\nmodels <- tibble(\n    a1 = runif(250, -40000, 40000),\n    a2 = runif(250, -5000, 10000)\n)\n\nmodels |> head()```\n\n### Cell 12\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000000d\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n`geom_abline()` draws a line from an intercept and a slope.```\n\n### Cell 13\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 6\nid: 0000000e\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_abline(aes(intercept = a1, slope = a2), data = models, alpha = 0.25) +\n    geom_point()```\n\n### Cell 14\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000000f\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nMost of these are terrible. A few are OK. **How do we say which line is best with a number?**```\n\n### Cell 15\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000010'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Distance between a model and the data\n\nStart with one \"model\" or *combination* of $a_1$ and $a_2$. For each person the model **predicts** `realinc`. The gap between a person's actual income and what the model predicted is the **residual**.\n\nLet's write the model as a function. `a` is a vector with our two numbers.```\n\n### Cell 16\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 7\nid: '00000011'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nmodel1 <- function(a, data) {\n    a[1] + data$educ * a[2]\n}\n\nmodel1(c(-5000, 3000), income_by_educ)```\n\n### Cell 17\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000012'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nHere is what those gaps look like for the line $a_1 = -5000$, $a_2 = 3000$. Each red segment is one residual.\n\n**Remember we are using the mean values by education level for visualization. Our actual model calculates the residual for every person in the data set!**```\n\n### Cell 18\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 8\nid: '00000013'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nincome_by_educ |>\n    mutate(pred = model1(c(-5000, 3000), income_by_educ)) |>\nggplot(aes(x = educ)) +\n    geom_abline(intercept = -5000, slope = 3000, color = 'grey40') +\n    geom_segment(aes(y = realinc, yend = pred), color = 'red') +\n    geom_point(aes(y = realinc))```\n\n### Cell 19\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000014'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nLet's pick a number that measures how well the model predicts `realinc`, not 67,000 numbers. We'll use the **root mean squared error** (RMSE):\n\n1. Get each residual: $\\text{actual} - \\text{predicted}$.\n2. **Square** them so all individual values are positive.\n3. Take the **mean**.\n4. Take the square **root**. Now the units are dollars again.\n\n$$ \\text{RMSE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n} \\sum (\\text{actual} - \\text{predicted})^2} $$```\n\n### Cell 20\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 9\nid: '00000015'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nmeasure_distance <- function(mod, data) {\n    diff <- data$realinc - model1(mod, data)\n    sqrt(mean(diff ^ 2))\n}\n\nmeasure_distance(c(-5000, 3000), gss)```\n\n### Cell 21\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000016'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nThat line misses by about $28,000 on a typical person. Smaller is better.\n\n**You do:** pick your own $a_1$ and $a_2$. Can you beat that distance?```\n\n### Cell 22\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 10\nid: '00000017'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nmeasure_distance(c(-15000, 3600), gss)```\n\n### Cell 23\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 00000018\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Score all 250 models\n\n`measure_distance()` takes one model at a time. `map2_dbl()` from **purrr** runs it once for each row of `models`. It walks down `a1` and `a2` together and returns $\\text{RMSE}$ for each pair.```\n\n### Cell 24\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 11\nid: 00000019\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ngss_dist <- function(a1, a2) {\n    measure_distance(c(a1, a2), gss)\n}\n\nmodels <- models |>\n    mutate(dist = map2_dbl(a1, a2, gss_dist))\n\nmodels |> arrange(dist) |> head()```\n\n### Cell 25\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000001a\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nPlot the 10 best. The lighter the line, the bigger the distance.```\n\n### Cell 26\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 12\nid: 0000001b\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_abline(\n        aes(intercept = a1, slope = a2, color = -dist),\n        data = models |> slice_min(dist, n = 10)\n    ) +\n    geom_point()```\n\n### Cell 27\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000001c\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n### Models as points\n\nEach model is just two numbers. So we can draw each model as a **point**, with $a_1$ on the x-axis and $a_2$ on the y-axis. Color by distance and circle the 10 best in red.```\n\n### Cell 28\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 13\nid: 0000001d\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(models, aes(x = a1, y = a2)) +\n    geom_point(data = models |> slice_min(dist, n = 10), size = 4, color = 'red') +\n    geom_point(aes(color = -dist))```\n\n### Cell 29\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000001e\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nThe good models are all in one spot. They are not spread out at random. Notice they follow a diagonal band: a lower intercept can make up for a steeper slope.```\n\n### Cell 30\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000001f\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Grid search\n\nRandom guessing is wasteful. Let's be smarter. Make an even **grid** of points around where the best models are, and score every one.\n\n`expand_grid()` makes every combination, like in the regression lecture.```\n\n### Cell 31\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 15\nid: '00000020'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ngrid <- expand_grid(\n    a1 = seq(-25000, -5000, length = 25),\n    a2 = seq(2500, 4500, length = 25)\n) |>\n    mutate(dist = map2_dbl(a1, a2, gss_dist))\n\ngrid |> arrange(dist) |> head()```\n\n### Cell 32\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 16\nid: '00000021'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(grid, aes(x = a1, y = a2)) +\n    geom_point(data = grid |> slice_min(dist, n = 10), size = 4, color = 'red') +\n    geom_point(aes(color = -dist))```\n\n### Cell 33\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 17\nid: '00000022'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_abline(\n        aes(intercept = a1, slope = a2, color = -dist),\n        data = grid |> slice_min(dist, n = 10)\n    ) +\n    geom_point()```\n\n### Cell 34\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000023'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nThe 10 best lines are almost on top of each other now. We could keep making the grid finer and finer until we zoom in on the best model.```\n\n### Cell 35\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000024'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Let the computer search\n\nThere is a better way. `optim()` is a general **optimizer**. Give it:\n\n- a starting guess\n- an *objective* to minimize, in our case this is `measure_distance`\n- anything else that the \"objective\" function needs (like the data: `data = gss`)\n\nIt makes a guess and checks the objective. If the objective goes down, it keeps making intelligent guesses while the objective goes downhill until it can't optimize any further.\n\nFor the starting guess, use our best random model.```\n\n### Cell 36\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 18\nid: '00000025'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nstart <- models |> slice_min(dist, n = 1)\n\nstart```\n\n### Cell 37\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 19\nid: '00000026'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nbest <- optim(c(start$a1, start$a2), measure_distance, data = gss)\n\nbest$par```\n\n### Cell 38\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 20\nid: '00000027'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nbest$value```\n\n### Cell 39\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 21\nid: 00000028\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_abline(intercept = best$par[1], slope = best$par[2], color = 'red') +\n    geom_point()```\n\n### Cell 40\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 00000029\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**You do:** start `optim()` at `c(0, 0)` instead. What happens?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer</summary>\n\n```r\noptim(c(0, 0), measure_distance, data = gss)$par\n```\n\nIt stops too early, at a worse model. Remember the diagonal band in the plot above? The ground is almost flat along it, so `optim()` thinks it is done. A good starting guess matters.\n\n</details>```\n\n### Cell 41\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 22\nid: 0000002a\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\noptim(c(0, 0), measure_distance, data = gss)$par```\n\n### Cell 42\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000002b\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n### Watch the search\n\n`optim()` tries many models on its way down. Let's write down every one.\n\n`measure_distance_log()` does the same job as `measure_distance()`. It also adds a row to `path` for each model it scores. The `<<-` saves to `path` outside the function.```\n\n### Cell 43\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 23\nid: 0000002c\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\npath <- tibble()\n\nmeasure_distance_log <- function(mod, data) {\n    dist <- measure_distance(mod, data)\n    path <<- bind_rows(path, tibble(a1 = mod[1], a2 = mod[2], dist = dist))\n    dist\n}```\n\n### Cell 44\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000002d\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nRun `optim()` from our best random model. Then number the steps.```\n\n### Cell 45\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 24\nid: 0000002e\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\npath <- tibble()\n\noptim(c(start$a1, start$a2), measure_distance_log, data = gss)$par\n\npath_good <- path |> mutate(step = row_number(), start = 'best random model')\n\npath_good |> head()```\n\n### Cell 46\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000031'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nTo see the hills and valleys, score a fine grid of models and draw **contour** lines. Each line connects models with the same distance, like a map of a mountain.```\n\n### Cell 47\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 25\nid: '00000032'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nsurface <- expand_grid(\n    a1 = seq(-25000, 5000, length = 60),\n    a2 = seq(0, 5000, length = 60)\n) |>\n    mutate(dist = map2_dbl(a1, a2, gss_dist))```\n\n### Cell 48\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000033'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nWe want more lines near the bottom of the valley, so we can see its shape. Start at the smallest distance and double the gap each time: 100, 200, 400, ...```\n\n### Cell 49\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 26\nid: '00000034'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nlevels <- min(surface$dist) + 100 * 2^(0:10)\n\nlevels```\n\n### Cell 50\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000035'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nNow draw the path from our best random model on the map. Each point is one model `optim()` tried.```\n\n### Cell 51\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 27\nid: '00000036'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(path_good, aes(x = a1, y = a2)) +\n    geom_contour(aes(z = dist), data = surface, breaks = levels, color = 'grey60') +\n    geom_path(color = 'grey30') +\n    geom_point(aes(color = step))```\n\n### Cell 52\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: '00000037'\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nThe valley is long and thin. `optim()` starts in the middle of it. It tries steps up and down, then walks left along the valley floor to the best model.```\n\n### Cell 53\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000003a\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n`optim()` does not care that our model is a line. It works for **any** function we can write that returns a distance. That is the big idea:\n\n1. Pick a **form** for your model.\n2. Pick a way to **measure** the \"fit\" from the data.\n3. **Search** for the $a_1$ and $a_2$ with the best fit.```\n\n### Cell 54\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000003b\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Compare with `linear_reg()`\n\nNow fit the model the way we did in the regression lecture.```\n\n### Cell 55\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 28\nid: 0000003c\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nfit1 <- linear_reg() |> fit(realinc ~ educ, data = gss)\n\nfit1```\n\n### Cell 56\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 29\nid: 0000003d\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nbest$par```\n\n### Cell 57\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000003e\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nSame numbers! The tiny difference is because `optim()` stops when it is \"close enough\".\n\n\n`linear_reg()` uses `lm()` under the hood. For straight-line models with this distance, there is a math shortcut that finds the exact answer in one step, no searching needed. That is why it is so fast. The name **least squares** comes from the objective function: *minimize the sum of the squared residuals.*```\n\n### Cell 58\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000003f\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Look at the predictions\n\n`predict()` gives the model's guess for new data. Make a grid of education levels and predict.```\n\n### Cell 59\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 30\nid: '00000040'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ngrid_educ <- tibble(educ = 0:20)\n\npreds <- fit1 |> predict(new_data = grid_educ) |> bind_cols(grid_educ)\n\npreds |> head()```\n\n### Cell 60\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 31\nid: '00000041'\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    geom_line(data = preds, aes(y = .pred), color = 'red')```\n\n### Cell 61\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000004a\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## You do: a different distance\n\nRMSE squares the residuals, so a few people with very high incomes pull hard on the line. Another choice is the **mean absolute error** (MAE): the mean of the absolute values of the residuals.\n\n1. Write `measure_distance_mae()`. Use `abs()` in place of the square and square root.\n2. Use `optim()` to find the best $a_1$ and $a_2$. Start at `best$par`.\n3. Draw both lines over `income_by_educ`. How are they different?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint</summary>\n\n```r\nmeasure_distance_mae <- function(mod, data) {\n    diff <- data$realinc - model1(mod, data)\n    mean(abs(diff))\n}\n```\n\n</details>```\n\n### Cell 62\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 32\nid: 0000004b\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nmeasure_distance_mae <- function(mod, data) {\n    diff <- data$realinc - model1(mod, data)\n    mean(abs(diff))\n}\n\nbest_mae <- optim(best$par, measure_distance_mae, data = gss)\n\nbest_mae$par```\n\n### Cell 63\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 33\nid: 0000004c\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(income_by_educ, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_abline(intercept = best$par[1], slope = best$par[2], color = 'red') +\n    geom_abline(intercept = best_mae$par[1], slope = best_mae$par[2], color = 'blue') +\n    geom_point()```\n\n### Cell 64\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 0000004d\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nThe blue MAE line sits lower. Income is **skewed**: a few people earn a lot. Squaring gives those big misses more pull, so the RMSE line moves up toward them. MAE gives them less pull.\n\nSame family, different distance, different \"best\" model. The numbers `tidy()` gives you depend on the distance you chose.```"
            },
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "notebooks/solutions/09-lecture-model-basics.ipynb"
            },
            "tool_call_id": "call_268ce319dd084b6788e2bc8d",
            "status": "completed",
            "kind": "other",
            "title": "Jupyter_MCP_Server_read_notebook"
          },
          {
            "kind": "other",
            "raw_output": {
              "output": "```yaml\nkernelspec:\n  display_name: R 4.5.3 (xr)\n  language: R\n  name: xr\nlanguage_info:\n  codemirror_mode: ''\n  file_extension: .R\n  mimetype: text/x-R\n  name: R\n  nbconvert_exporter: ''\n  pygments_lexer: r\n  version: 4.5.3\n```\n\n### Cell 0\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 13\nid: 0611cd27-b7bd-4202-ab12-228eca2944e2\nscrolled: true\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nlibrary('tidymodels')\n\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)```\n\n### Cell 1\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 14\nid: 37254b79-83b4-4269-8823-d149463070fc\nscrolled: true\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ngss = read.csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/gss_subset.csv')\n\ngss <- gss |> drop_na(realinc, educ)\n\ngss |> glimpse()```\n\n### Cell 2\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 15\nid: 2059b77c-e8b4-48aa-925e-c81563a11dc5\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(gss, aes(x = educ, y = realinc)) +\n    geom_jitter(alpha = 0.02, width = 0.3)```\n\n### Cell 3\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 16\nid: d93f0a42-efa7-4a1a-957a-b6a2ae6f4735\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nset.seed(512)\n\nn_models <- 200\n\nparam_grid <- tibble(\n  intercept = runif(n_models, 0, 60000),\n  slope     = runif(n_models, -5000, 5000)\n)\n\nparam_grid```\n\n### Cell 4\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 17\nid: 168bbbcf-0711-47af-a564-4c05f07f32fc\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\neduc_means <- gss |>\n    group_by(educ) |>\n    summarize(mean_realinc = mean(realinc), .groups = \"drop\")\n\neduc_means |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = educ, y = mean_realinc)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    geom_abline(\n        data = param_grid,\n        aes(intercept = intercept, slope = slope),\n        color = \"steelblue\",\n        alpha = 0.05\n    )```\n\n### Cell 5\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 20\nid: 1a07b0d8-5805-4300-8c85-d16581b43c85\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nmodel_scores <- educ_means |>\n    cross_join(param_grid |> mutate(model_id = row_number())) |>\n    mutate(predicted = intercept + slope * educ) |>\n    group_by(model_id, intercept, slope) |>\n    summarize(rmse = sqrt(mean((mean_realinc - predicted)^2)), .groups = \"drop\")\n\nmodel_scores |>\n    arrange(rmse) |>\n    slice_head(n = 5)```\n\n### Cell 6\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 21\nid: f1445900-3bd1-40fa-96c2-311ae4e1cf1e\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ncalc_rmse <- function(intercept, slope, data) {\n    predicted <- intercept + slope * data$educ\n    sqrt(mean((data$mean_realinc - predicted)^2))\n}```\n\n### Cell 7\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 22\nid: dadea29d-4722-4e61-8054-448c2d20fa5e\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nparam_grid <- param_grid |>\n    mutate(rmse = map2_dbl(intercept, slope, calc_rmse, data = educ_means))\n\nparam_grid```\n\n### Cell 8\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 23\nid: 9ed91082-8092-4ac5-8742-10101c0e9977\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nbest_model <- param_grid |>\n    slice_min(rmse, n = 1)\n\neduc_means |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = educ, y = mean_realinc)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    geom_abline(\n        data = best_model,\n        aes(intercept = intercept, slope = slope),\n        color = \"steelblue\"\n    )```\n\n### Cell 9\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: f81176ce-ef2e-471e-86b1-f798dcf96452\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nSteps for creating a model:\n1. Pick the model form: a linear model\n\n$$ \\text{realinc}_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\cdot \\text{educ}_i + \\varepsilon_i $$\n\n2. Design a way to evaluate different model parameters.\n3. Optimize our parameter values.```\n\n### Cell 10\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 28\nid: 8575551b-9b14-48e3-b43c-a964b6c3e71c\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nintercept = 2000\nslope = 3000\n\npred = intercept + slope * gss$educ\nresid  = gss$realinc - pred\nmean(resid^2)\nsqrt(mean(resid^2, na.rm = TRUE))```\n\n### Cell 11\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 29\nid: 56174e0d-a480-4df9-9e76-808edfff7b0d\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\neduc_means |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = educ, y = mean_realinc)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    geom_abline(\n        intercept = intercept,\n        slope = slope,\n        color = \"steelblue\"\n    )```\n\n### Cell 12\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 30\nid: 3c2d4344-235d-4c39-96b2-06ac205a3046\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\neduc_means |>\n    mutate(predicted = intercept + slope * educ) |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = educ, y = mean_realinc)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    geom_abline(\n        intercept = intercept,\n        slope = slope,\n        color = \"steelblue\"\n    ) +\n    geom_segment(\n        aes(xend = educ, yend = predicted),\n        color = \"firebrick\"\n    )```\n\n### Cell 13\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cc1a9bd2-ff39-4866-b44e-540cc4f81e2a\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 14\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 31\nid: 442ea6ec-a54e-4862-88d4-63cb56dc9643\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ntop_models <- param_grid |>\n    slice_min(rmse, n = 5)\n\neduc_means |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = educ, y = mean_realinc)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    geom_abline(\n        data = top_models,\n        aes(intercept = intercept, slope = slope, color = rmse)\n    )```",
